#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176820} :activity/id #uuid "4cf8b9a9-4050-5d59-b451-2f66b157c8dd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050943 :topic/id #uuid "73c67a70-178b-5e99-8871-46ff25f570ba" :topic/math-academy-id 3593} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177044 :tutorial/content "**Speed** is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*.\n\nWe can calculate the speed of a moving object using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a car travels $80$ miles in $2$ hours at a constant rate. What is the speed of the car? \n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $80,$ by the number of hours, $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{2} = 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $40$ miles per hour." :tutorial/id #uuid "e151f782-1f59-5438-92a6-e678c9041d99" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5959 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "136b294f-0279-574d-9755-cfc8cb54f15d" :step/math-academy-id 36386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176030 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175885 :question/id #uuid "696213fa-757d-5450-906a-ffb0638599e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88babe91-d8bf-53ba-8d72-35ad35f9253e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050273}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268982}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of an Object"} :step/id #uuid "aed82a50-e10d-539e-895f-797eb5220670" :step/math-academy-id 36387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176031 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175886 :question/id #uuid "1c7eac8b-b163-532f-84f0-10ffd23a67f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "534de76f-d4bc-55b5-858e-2b594f63b7bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050285}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500339}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distance Given Speed and Time"} :step/id #uuid "2e52190a-967b-5eee-8781-4b1c6cdc1bf7" :step/math-academy-id 36388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176032 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175887 :question/id #uuid "cb7d1f42-a5bc-5fba-834c-07a2ceb0644a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9706"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6570253a-b05a-5721-9108-5fddab2b5388" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500355}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Time Given Distance and Speed"} :step/id #uuid "cb434a73-533d-5a74-927b-b8b1bf69153e" :step/math-academy-id 36389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177045 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the example of a car traveling at ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles per hour. \n\nIf the car travels at this speed, it covers  a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles *every hour*. Therefore, we can deduce the following:\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hour, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{1}} = 40$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{2}} = 80$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{3}} = 120$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{4}} = 160$ miles\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nThis suggests the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at a constant speed.\n\nFor instance, suppose an elevator traveled for $1.5$ minutes at a constant speed of $120$ feet per minute. What distance is covered by the elevator?\n\nThe speed is $120$ feet per minute. To find the distance covered in $1.5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $1.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =120\\times 1.5 \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elevator covered a distance of $180$ feet." :tutorial/id #uuid "95f37ad7-7647-57e9-ae85-7a3dcab40f25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5960 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Distance Traveled by an Object"} :step/id #uuid "02f9ad99-d474-59a2-b870-d4cc8af16598" :step/math-academy-id 36390 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177046 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula relating distance to speed and time:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{distance}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\text{distance} \\div \\text{speed}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the time taken for an object moving at a particular speed to cover a certain distance. \n\nFor example, suppose a plane flew for $3\\,120$ kilometers at a constant speed of $520$ kilometers per hour. For how long was the plane in motion?\n\nTo determine the time the plane was in motion, we divide the distance, $3\\,120$ kilometers, by the speed, $520$ kilometers per hour. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120÷10}{520÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{52} \\\\  & =312÷52 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane was in motion for $6$ hours." :tutorial/id #uuid "76064dfb-b2e4-56a5-95f7-2a97b1fb392b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5961 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Total Time an Object is in Motion"} :step/id #uuid "1aec8595-c434-5eab-92bb-a1d9a3d99a15" :step/math-academy-id 36391 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176823}}] :activity/title "Speed as a Unit Rate" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176136}]}
